   "properties": {
    "id": "T08",
    "nom": "The return by the Illinois River",
    "debut": 1673.5411,
    "fin": 1673.7329,
    "date_edtf": "1673-07-17/1673-09-25",
    "certitude": "approximative",
    "mode": "fleuve",
    "description": "On July 17, 1673, the canoes leave Akansea and struggle back up against the current. At about 38 degrees they take the Illinois River, the most beautiful and convenient they have seen, lined with prairies and woods. They stop for three days with the Peoria. A Kaskaskia chief and his young men then guide them to the Lake of the Illinois (Michigan) by the Chicago portage, and they follow the west shore of the lake to the Baie des Puants.",
    "sources": "Récit des voyages du P. Marquette (1855), p. 90-92; Gagnon, Louis Jolliet (2e éd., 1913), p. 125-133.",
    "note": "",
    "type": "trajet"
